-- #797 (S2) — the LOAD-BEARING NEGATIVE half of the #797 fix.  #797 said a
-- top-level signature's declared free return-position effect var (`f : Int -> <e>
-- String`) collapsed to the closed `<>` in the binding-boundary escape check
-- (`declaredEffects` dropped `TyEffect`'s tail field), so an honest effectful body
-- was rejected with a diagnostic that FALSELY read "declared with <>".  The fix
-- makes that check skip a genuinely open row.
--
-- 🚨 "Genuinely open" is NOT "syntactically open".  `e` HERE also rides an
-- ARGUMENT (`(Int -> <e> Int)`), so the CALLER — not the body — picks it, and a
-- caller may pick `<>`.  A body adding <Stdout> to it therefore still LAUNDERS
-- (the no-laundering law, docs/spec/EFFECTS-SEMANTICS.md §5), and must still be
-- rejected.  Widening the escape check by tail-presence ALONE would have turned
-- this program from REJECT into ACCEPT — loud to silent, a severity INCREASE — so
-- this fixture, not the drained #797 pin, is what holds the fix to its scope.
--
-- The message must also stay honest in this surviving arm: it now reads
-- "declared with <e>", the row actually written, not the false "<>".  Exit codes
-- cannot see that; it is pinned by inspection, and by this file's existence.
g : (Int -> <e> Int) -> <e> Int
g k =
  let _ = putStr "leak"
  k 1

main = println (g (x => x))
